  <ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="Tag855" contextRef="C1">&lt;div&gt;&lt;!--?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="no"?--&gt;
&lt;div style="line-height: 108%; font-family: Calibri; font-size: 11pt;"&gt;
&lt;div class="Section_1"&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: center; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold;"&gt;MarkWest Pioneer,&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold;"&gt; &amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold;"&gt;L.L.C.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 6pt; margin-bottom: 0pt; text-align: center; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold;"&gt;Notes to Financial Statements&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 6pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: center; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold;"&gt;($ in thousands)&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold;"&gt;1. Organization and Business&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 12pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;MarkWest Pioneer, L.L.C. (the &amp;#8220;Company&amp;#8221;) was formed on&lt;/font&gt; &lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;September 20, 2007 under the provisions of the Delaware Limited Liability Company Act. The Company owns and operates the Arkoma Connector Pipeline (&amp;#8220;Arkoma Connector&amp;#8221;), a 50-mile pipeline regulated by the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (&amp;#8220;FERC&amp;#8221;) that interconnects with the Midcontinent Express Pipeline, Gulf Crossing Pipeline and the Natural Gas Pipeline of America L.L.C.&amp;#8217;s pipeline near Bennington, Oklahoma providing a market outlet for natural gas produced in the Woodford Shale. Construction of the Arkoma Connector began in December 2008 and operations commenced in July 2009. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;The Company is owned by MarkWest Energy Operating Company, L.L.C. (&amp;#8220;MarkWest&amp;#8221;), a subsidiary of MPLX LP.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold;"&gt;2. Significant Accounting Policies&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Basis of Presentation&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;The accompanying financial statements of the Company have been prepared in accordance with the accounting requirements of FERC as set forth in its applicable Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting other than accounting principles generally accepted in the United States of America (&amp;#8220;GAAP&amp;#8221;). &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;The preparation of financial statements requires management to make estimates and assumptions that affect the reported amounts of assets and liabilities and the disclosure of contingent assets and liabilities as of the date of the financial statements and the related reported amounts of revenues and expenses during the respective reporting periods. Estimates are subject to uncertainties due to the levels of subjectivity and judgment necessary to account for highly uncertain matters or the susceptibility of such matters to change and affect items such as those made in areas of FERC regulations, evaluating impairments of long-lived assets; establishing estimated useful lives for long-lived assets; estimating revenues, expense accruals and capital expenditures; valuing asset retirement obligations; and determining liabilities, if any, for environmental and legal contingencies. Actual results could differ materially from those estimates.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Customer Accounts Receivables &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;Customer accounts receivables consist of customer accounts receivable, which are recorded at the invoiced amount and generally do not bear interest.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt; &amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;Past-due balances over&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt; &amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;150 days and other higher risk amounts&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt; &amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;are reviewed individually for collectability.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt; &amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;Balances that remain outstanding after reasonable collection efforts have been unsuccessful are written off through a charge to a valuation allowance and a credit to accounts receivable. Management reviews the allowance quarterly. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Utility Plant&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;Utility plant consists primarily of natural gas pipeline assets, compressors, and related facilities that are recorded at cost. The recorded cost of the pipelines and related equipment includes an Allowance for Funds Used During Construction (&amp;#8220;AFUDC&amp;#8221;), which represents the estimated cost of the debt and equity required to finance the construction of the regulated assets. The pipeline and related equipment are depreciated on the straight-line basis under the composite method of depreciation. Depreciation is provided using a FERC composite rate of 2.6%.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;Under the composite method, upon the disposition of pipeline, plant and equipment, the cost less net proceeds is charged to accumulated depreciation and no gain or loss is recognized except in the case of retirements or sales of entire regulated operating units or systems. Expenditures that extend the useful lives of assets are capitalized. Repairs, maintenance and renewals that do not extend the useful lives of assets are expensed as incurred. Such assets are reviewed for impairment whenever events or changes in circumstances indicate that the carrying amount of an asset may not be recoverable. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;When operating units or systems are sold or otherwise disposed of, any gains or losses are reported in the Statements of Income. Gains on the disposal of property and equipment are recognized when they occur, which is generally at the time of closing. If a loss on disposal is expected, such losses are recognized when the assets are classified as held for sale.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Asset Retirement Obligations&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;An asset retirement obligation (&amp;#8220;ARO&amp;#8221;) is a legal obligation associated with the retirement of tangible long lived assets that generally result from the acquisition, construction, development or normal operation of the asset. AROs are recorded at fair value in the period in which they are incurred, if a reasonable estimate of fair value can be made and added to the carrying amount of the associated asset. This additional carrying amount is then depreciated over the life of the asset. The liability is determined using a credit adjusted risk-free interest rate and increases due to the passage of time based on the time value of money until the obligation is settled. AROs have not been recognized for certain assets because the fair value cannot be reasonably estimated since the settlement dates of the obligations are indeterminate. Such obligations will be recognized in the period when sufficient information becomes available to estimate a range of potential settlement dates. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Regulatory Assets and Liabilities&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;The economic effects of regulation can result in a regulated company recording assets for costs that have been or are expected to be approved for recovery from customers or recording liabilities for amounts that are expected to be returned to customers or for instances where the regulator provides current rates that are intended to recover costs that are expected to be incurred in the future. Accordingly, the Company records assets and liabilities that result from the regulated ratemaking process that may not be recorded under GAAP for non-regulated entities. The Company assesses whether any regulatory assets are probable of future recovery by considering factors such as applicable regulatory changes and recent rate orders to other regulated entities.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Revenue Recognition&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;Revenue is measured based on consideration specified in a contract with a customer. The Company recognizes revenue when it satisfies performance obligations by transferring control over a product or providing services to a customer. Performance obligations are determined based on the specific terms of the arrangements and the services offered and whether they are distinct.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;The Company provides services under fee-based arrangements. Under fee-based arrangements, the Company receives a fee or fees for transportation services provided to its customers. The revenue that the Company earns from these arrangements is generally directly related to the volume of natural gas that flows through the Company&amp;#8217;s transportation system and is not directly dependent on commodity prices. Revenue is recognized over time when the performance obligation is satisfied as services are provided in a series. The Company has elected to use the output measure of progress to recognize revenue based on the units gathered. The transaction price is based on variable components which are primarily dependent on volumes. These fee-based arrangements are reported as &lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-style: italic;"&gt;Gas Operating Revenues&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt; on the accompanying Statements of Income. Customers generally pay monthly based on the services performed that month.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Natural Gas Imbalances&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;Natural gas imbalances represent volumes owed to or due from the Company resulting from differences in volumes received from and delivered for customers. These differences are settled with affiliates of the Company and third parties according to the terms of their operational balancing agreements (&amp;#8220;OBA&amp;#8221;) and are valued at market index prices as of the balance sheet date. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Income Taxes&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;The Company is a subsidiary of MPLX, LP, a master limited partnership (&amp;#8220;MLP&amp;#8221;). For tax purposes under the provisions of the Internal Revenue Code the Company is treated as a partnership and does not pay income taxes. The FERC&amp;#8217;s 2005 Income Tax Policy Statement allowed pass through entities, such as the Company, to recover an income tax allowance. Therefore, the Company recognized income taxes in its financial statements. On January 1, 2018, the Company adopted FERC&amp;#8217;s Revised Policy Statement on Treatment of Income Taxes issued on March 15, 2018 along with subsequent guidance issued on July 18, 2018 (&amp;#8220;Revised Policy Statement&amp;#8221;). As a result, effective January 1, 2018 the Company prospectively eliminated its tax allowance, along with its Accumulated Deferred Income Taxes (&amp;#8220;ADIT&amp;#8221;) and tax related regulatory assets. The Company is not waiving its right to seek a tax allowance in the future as a result of its compliance with FERC&amp;#8217;s accounting guidance.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Environmental Costs&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;Environmental expenditures are capitalized if the costs mitigate or prevent future contamination or if the costs improve environmental safety or efficiency of the existing assets. The Company recognizes remediation costs and penalties when the responsibility to remediate is probable and the amount of associated costs can be reasonably estimated. The timing of remediation accruals coincides with completion of a feasibility study or the commitment to a formal plan of action. Remediation liabilities are accrued based on estimates of known environmental exposure. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold; font-style: italic;"&gt;Fair Value of Financial Instruments&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;Management believes the carrying amounts of financial instruments, including trade receivables, affiliate receivables and payables, affiliate note receivable, accounts payable, and accrued liabilities approximate fair value because of the short-term maturity of these instruments.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold;"&gt;3. Affiliate Transactions&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;The Company has no employees. Operating, maintenance and general and administrative services, including insurance, are provided to the Company under service agreements with affiliates of MarkWest. The total affiliate operating expenses for the year ended December 31, 2025 and 2024 were $1.5 million and $1.6 million, respectively.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;MarkWest collects customer accounts receivables on behalf of the Company.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; -aw-import: spaces;"&gt;&amp;#160; &lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;The Company also has an OBA in place with an affiliate of MarkWest to settle pipeline imbalances. From time to time, the Company may sell to or purchase from MarkWest affiliates, assets and inventory at the lesser of average unit cost or fair value.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="width: 36pt; font-family: 'Times New Roman'; display: inline-block; -aw-tabstop-align: left; -aw-tabstop-pos: 36pt;"&gt; &amp;#160;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;From January 1 through September 30, 2024, the Company was party to a cash management agreement (the &amp;#8220;Account Agreement&amp;#8221;) with an affiliate. Monies advanced to the Company under the Account Agreement were considered loans, accruing interest and repayable upon demand without penalty based on a rate equal to the one-month term, Term SOFR Rate (&amp;#8220;SOFR Rate&amp;#8221;) for US Dollar deposits, plus a margin of 0.50%. The Company received interest on monies advanced to the affiliate at a rate equal to the one-month term, SOFR Rate for US Dollar deposits, plus a margin of 0.00%. Interest was calculated on the net balance on the last day of each month based on actual days elapsed and on the basis of a 360-day year and became part of the affiliate note balance. The Account Agreement was terminated on September 30, 2024 and the Company entered into a new cash management agreement (the &amp;#8220;Agreement&amp;#8221;) with an affiliate on October 1, 2024, which converted the then note receivable balance of $18.3 million to the initial note receivable balance under the new Agreement. No interest shall accrue on any of the outstanding balances and amounts due or receivable are entitled to be repaid on demand without premium or penalty. Under both the Account Agreement and the Agreement, on a monthly basis the net affiliate activity is added to or deducted from the affiliate note receivable balance. At December 31, 2025 and 2024, the Company had an affiliate note receivable balance of $5.6 million and $17.6 million, respectively. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 12pt; margin-bottom: 12pt; text-align: justify; page-break-inside: avoid; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-weight: bold;"&gt;4. Utility Plant&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p style="margin-top: 0pt; margin-bottom: 6pt; text-indent: 36pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&lt;font style="font-family: 'Times New Roman';"&gt;Utility plant is comprised of the following as of:&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
